{"id":"3c7253f4-efed-4c31-b040-8da91ba209a0","updated_at":"2025-05-12T09:30:29.385Z","updated_by":"elastic","created_at":"2025-05-03T10:15:32.524Z","created_by":"elastic","name":"Suricata Alert: Network Trojan","tags":["Domain: Network","Use Case: Threat Detection","Tactic: Command and Control","Tactic: Initial Access","Data Source: Suricata","Type: Custom"],"interval":"5m","enabled":true,"revision":2,"description":"Detects network activity matching known Trojan signatures reported by Suricata. This may indicate malware infection, C2 communication, or malicious payload delivery on the network.","risk_score":73,"severity":"high","note":"- Review the suricata.alert.signature and network.transport fields to understand the nature of the detected traffic (e.g., protocol used, payload type, signature match).\n- Inspect source.ip and destination.ip to determine the origin and target of the suspicious connection. Cross-reference with known threat intelligence sources.\n- Analyze the network.direction to understand whether the traffic was inbound, outbound, or lateral within the internal network.\n- Investigate the host.name and associated user session at the time of alert to determine potential compromise or unauthorized activity.\n- Check for correlated endpoint detections (from Elastic Defend or similar tools) around the time of the Suricata alert that could indicate malicious process execution or persistence.\n- Identify whether the Suricata rule triggered on payloads commonly associated with malware loaders, exploit kits, or C2 channels.\n- Review DNS, HTTP, or TLS metadata for evidence of suspicious domains, JA3 hashes, or known bad IPs.","license":"","output_index":"","meta":{"kibana_siem_app_url":"http://192.168.1.36:5601/app/security"},"author":[],"false_positives":[],"from":"now-14m","rule_id":"400dc56e-59a7-47a3-941c-b095ce949339","max_signals":1,"risk_score_mapping":[],"severity_mapping":[],"threat":[{"framework":"MITRE ATT&CK","tactic":{"id":"TA0011","name":"Com